+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

deadman works with httpd(8), slowcgi(8), cron(8) and smtpd(8) from the
base system. The examples below use deadman.example.org as the name for
the ping URLs.

slowcgi
=======

	# rcctl enable slowcgi
	# rcctl start slowcgi

httpd
=====

Give the ping URLs a name of their own. If its DNS is on a CDN, make the
record DNS only: the CDN's proxy would see every token.

httpd will not load a TLS server whose certificate does not exist yet, so
first get a certificate for the name with acme-client(1), using a server
on port 80 like the first one in /etc/examples/httpd.conf. Then add a
server for the pings to /etc/httpd.conf, and reload httpd:

	server "deadman.example.org" {
		listen on * tls port 443
		tls {
			certificate "/etc/ssl/deadman.example.org.fullchain.pem"
			key "/etc/ssl/private/deadman.example.org.key"
		}
		location "/ping/*" {
			fastcgi socket "/run/slowcgi.sock"
			root "/cgi-bin/deadman.cgi"
		}
		location * {
			block return 404
		}
	}

acme-client exits 2 when there is nothing to renew, so renew from
/etc/daily.local rather than behind cron's -n flag:

	acme-client deadman.example.org && rcctl reload httpd

cron
====

Run deadman every minute, one at a time, with mail to root only if it
fails, from root's crontab(5):

	*	*	*	*	*	-sn ${PREFIX}/sbin/deadman

Checks
======

Make a token with "deadman token", and add a line for the check to
${SYSCONFDIR}/deadman.conf, as described in deadman.conf(5). Within a
minute, deadman makes the check's ping files, and its URL starts working:

	$ ftp -V -o - https://deadman.example.org/ping/TOKEN
	OK
	# deadman status

Jobs ping that URL when they succeed, and may add /start when they start
and /fail when they fail. deadman mails a check's address when it goes
down and when it comes back up.
